---
import HelpSection from "../../components/HelpSection.astro"
import Kbd from "../../components/Kbd.astro"
import Keys from "../../components/Keys.astro"
import Help from "../../layouts/Help.astro"
---

<Help
    title="Using Rollport"
    description="Choose what to import and how files are named, stop and continue an import, import again later, change the folder, and use the keyboard."
>
    <div class="max-w-(--container-text)">
        <h1 class="text-3xl font-semibold">Using Rollport</h1>
        <p class="mt-4 text-lg text-muted text-pretty">
            What you can change, and what happens on the next import.
        </p>
    </div>

    <HelpSection
        id="options"
        screen="options"
        alt="Rollport with Options open: date taken, media types, and file names"
    >
        <p>
            Open <b>Options</b> at the bottom of the window. Each choice applies
            at once, and the count of what will be imported changes with it.
            Press <Kbd>Esc</Kbd> or click outside to close Options.
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <b>Date taken</b>: <b>Any date</b>, <b>Last 7 days</b>,{" "}
                <b>Last 30 days</b>, <b>Last 12 months</b>, or{" "}
                <b>Between two dates</b>. "Last 7 days" is today and the six
                days before it, and both dates of "Between two dates" are
                included. The date range is not saved: Rollport starts at Any
                date each time you open it, so a range you forgot about never
                leaves photos behind.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b>Media types</b>: <b>All media</b>,{" "}
                <b>No Live Photo videos</b> (the short clip that goes with each
                Live Photo), <b>Photos only</b>, or <b>Videos only</b>. Photos
                only leaves out every video, Live Photo clips too. Videos only
                leaves out the Live Photo clips. This choice is saved with the
                folder, and you can change it at any time.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b>File names</b>: saved with the folder, and set for good at
                its first import. See{" "}
                <a href="#file-names">How files are named</a>.
            </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            A new folder starts with <b>Any date</b>, <b>All media</b>, and{" "}
            <b>Date and time in the name</b>.
        </p>
    </HelpSection>

    <HelpSection id="file-names">
        <p>
            Until a folder's first import, you can choose how its files are
            named:
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <b>Date and time in the name</b>:{" "}
                <code>2026-09-02_15-04-11_IMG_0001.HEIC</code>
            </li>
            <li>
                <b>Year and month folders</b>:{" "}
                <code>2026/09/IMG_0001.HEIC</code>
            </li>
            <li>
                <b>The phone's own name</b>: <code>IMG_0001.HEIC</code>
            </li>
            <li>
                <b>A pattern of your own</b>: see below.
            </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            After the first import, the folder keeps that naming, so all its
            files are named the same way. Options then shows the naming with a
            lock.
        </p>
        <p>
            The date in a name is when the photo was taken, in this computer's
            time zone. If two files would get the same name, the second gets{" "}
            <code>_1</code> before its extension, the next <code>_2</code>, and
            so on.
        </p>
        <p>
            To write a pattern of your own, choose <b>A pattern of your own</b>{" "}
            and type it, then press <Kbd>Enter</Kbd>:
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <code>{"{name}"}</code> is the iPhone's name for the file, with
                its extension, such as <code>IMG_0001.HEIC</code>. Every pattern
                needs it.
            </li>
            <li>
                <code>{"{mtime:%Y-%m-%d}"}</code> is the date the photo was
                taken. Between the colon and the brace, <code>%Y</code> is the
                year, <code>%m</code> the month, <code>%d</code> the day,{" "}
                <code>%H</code> the hour, <code>%M</code> the minute, and{" "}
                <code>%S</code> the second.
            </li>
            <li>
                <code>/</code> makes a folder. Rollport makes the folders it
                needs.
            </li>
            <li>Any other text is used as it is.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            For example, <code>{"{mtime:%Y}/{mtime:%m-%d}_{name}"}</code> gives{" "}
            <code>2026/09-02_IMG_0001.HEIC</code>. Rollport says at once when a
            pattern cannot work, for example when it has no{" "}
            <code>{"{name}"}</code>, or when it uses characters that Windows
            does not allow in file names. Rollport refuses those characters on
            every system, so the folder still works if you move it to Windows.
            An empty pattern goes back to the naming you had before.
        </p>
    </HelpSection>

    <HelpSection
        id="copying"
        screen="importing"
        alt="Rollport is 63 percent done, with about a minute left"
    >
        <p>
            The photos go past as they are copied, and Rollport shows how much
            is done, how many files and how much data are left, and the time
            left.
        </p>
        <p>
            You can click <b>Stop</b> at any time: what is already copied stays
            in the folder, and the next import goes on from there. A file that
            was being copied when you stopped is removed, and copied again next
            time.
        </p>
        <p>
            If you unplug the iPhone during an import, the import stops in the
            same way. Plug it in again and click <b>Continue</b>.
        </p>
        <p>
            Rollport tries each file three times. A file that still cannot be
            copied does not stop the import: Rollport names it at the end, and
            it stays on the iPhone for the next import.
        </p>
        <p>
            While an import runs, you cannot change the iPhone, the folder, or
            Options. Only one import at a time can write into a folder, also
            from another computer, where the drive supports it.
        </p>
    </HelpSection>

    <HelpSection
        id="again"
        screen="done"
        alt="Rollport says Imported 312, and that nothing was deleted from the iPhone"
    >
        <p>
            When the import is done, Rollport says what it copied, and you can
            unplug the iPhone. Below that, it shows the next step:
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <b>Continue</b> imports what is left after you stopped, or after
                the iPhone was unplugged.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b>Check again</b> looks for new photos, for example ones you
                took since.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b>Show in Finder</b> (Mac), <b>Show in Explorer</b> (Windows),
                or <b>Show in file manager</b> (Linux) opens the folder.
            </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            Next time, connect the iPhone and click <b>Import</b>: only what is
            new is copied. When there is nothing new, Rollport says{" "}
            <b>Up to date</b> and when the last import was.
        </p>
        <p>
            The folder keeps a record of what it already has, in a small file
            named <code>rollport-library.db</code>. Keep that file. Without it,
            Rollport recognizes only the files that are still where it put them,
            with the default naming, and copies everything else again. Moving or
            renaming the photos in the folder is fine while the file is there.
        </p>
        <p>
            Rollport never deletes anything on the iPhone. Once the photos are
            in the folder, you can delete them on the iPhone yourself to free up
            space.
        </p>
    </HelpSection>

    <HelpSection id="folders">
        <p>
            To use another folder, click the folder name at the top of the
            window, or drop a folder anywhere on the window. Rollport uses it at
            once.
        </p>
        <p>
            You can import into as many folders as you like. Each folder keeps
            its own record, so each one gets only what it does not have yet.
        </p>
        <p>
            Each iPhone remembers its folder: when you connect it, Rollport
            opens the folder it last imported into. An iPhone that never
            imported is offered the folder in use. With more than one iPhone
            connected, click the iPhone's name at the top of the window to
            choose one.
        </p>
    </HelpSection>

    <HelpSection id="keys">
        <p>
            On a Mac, use <Kbd>⌘</Kbd>. On Windows and Linux, use{" "}
            <Kbd>Ctrl</Kbd>.
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <Keys mac={["⌘", "Return"]} pc={["Ctrl", "Enter"]} />: Import,
                or the main button after an import (Continue, Import, or Check
                again).
            </li>
            <li>
                <Keys mac={["⌘", "."]} pc={["Ctrl", "."]} />: stop the import.
            </li>
            <li>
                <Kbd>Esc</Kbd>: close Options.
            </li>
            <li>
                <Keys mac={["⌘", "+"]} pc={["Ctrl", "+"]} />: make the window
                larger. <Keys mac={["⌘", "−"]} pc={["Ctrl", "−"]} />: smaller.{" "}
                <Keys mac={["⌘", "0"]} pc={["Ctrl", "0"]} />: back to actual
                size. The window grows with its contents, up to twice the size,
                as far as the screen allows. Rollport remembers the size.
            </li>
            <li>
                <Keys mac={["⌘", "?"]} /> on a Mac, or <Kbd>F1</Kbd> on Windows
                and Linux: open this help. The <b>?</b> button at the bottom of
                the window does the same; point at it to see the version of
                Rollport.
            </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            On a Mac, the View menu has the sizes, and the Help menu has the
            help.
        </p>
    </HelpSection>

    <HelpSection id="remembered">
        <p>
            Rollport itself remembers only three things: the folder you used
            last, the folder of each iPhone, and the window size. It keeps them
            in <code>settings.json</code> in its settings folder:
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                Mac:{" "}
                <code>
                    ~/Library/Application Support/com.artificiadrian.rollport
                </code>
            </li>
            <li>
                Windows: <code>%APPDATA%\com.artificiadrian.rollport</code>
            </li>
            <li>
                Linux: <code>~/.config/com.artificiadrian.rollport</code>
            </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            The file names no iPhone: each one is stored as a scrambled code (a
            hash), not by its name or serial number.
        </p>
        <p>
            Everything about a folder is kept in the folder: what it already
            has, its media types, and its naming, in{" "}
            <code>rollport-library.db</code>. So a folder you move to another
            disk or computer keeps working. Rollport writes nothing into a
            folder until its first import. Beside the record you may see{" "}
            <code>rollport-library.db-wal</code>,{" "}
            <code>rollport-library.db-shm</code>, and a hidden{" "}
            <code>.rollport-lock</code>; leave them there.
        </p>
    </HelpSection>
</Help>
